==English==
{{rfc|en|redundant definitions list}}
{{wp}}
{{wikiquote|Spring (season)}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sprĭng|a=RP,GA}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈspɹɪŋ/|[ˈspɹʷɪŋ]}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-spring.ogg|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-spring.wav|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-spring.ogg|a=GA}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈspɹiŋ/|[ˈspɹʷiŋ]|a=Upper Midwestern US,Western US,CA,pre-/ŋ/ tensing}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈspɹɪ̝ŋ/|[ˈspɹʷɪ̝ŋ]|a=AU}}
** {{audio|en|En-au-spring.ogg}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋ|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|spring}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|springen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|springan|t=to spring, leap, bounce, sprout forth, emerge, spread out}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*springan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*springaną|t=to burst forth}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*spre(n)ǵʰ-|t=to move, race, spring}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*sperǵʰ-|t=to hurry}}.

{{col-top|2|cog}}
* {{cog|stq|springe}}
* {{cog|fy|springe}}
* {{cog|nl|springen}}
* {{cog|nds-de|springen}}
* {{cog|de|springen}}
* {{cog|da|springe}}
* {{cog|sv|springa}}
* {{cog|no|springe}}
* {{cog|fo|springa}}
* {{cog|is|springa|t=to burst, explode}}.
{{col-bottom}}

Other possible cognates include {{cog|lt|spreñgti||to [[push]] (in)}}, {{cog|cu|прѧсти||to [[spin]], to [[stretch]]}}, {{cog|la|spargere||to [[sprinkle]], to [[scatter]]}}, {{cog|grc|σπέρχω||to [[hasten]]}}, {{cog|sa|स्पृहयति|tr=spṛháyati||to be [[eager]]}}. Some newer senses derived from the noun.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|sprang,sprung|sprung}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move or burst forth.
#: {{ux|en|The boat [[spring a leak|'''sprang''' a leak]] and began to sink.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1540|translator=John Bellenden|author=Livy|title=History of Rome|section=Vol. I, i, xxii, p. 125
|passage=...[[the|þe]] wound [[that|þat]] was '''springand''' with huge [[streams|stremes]] of [[blood|blude]]...}}
## To [[appear]].
##* {{RQ:KJV|Judges|19|25|passage=...so the man [[took]]e his concubine, and brought her [[forth|foorth]] [[unto|vnto]] them, and they knew her, and abused her all the night [[until|vntil]] the morning: and when the day began to '''spring''', they let her [[go]]e.}}
##* {{RQ:Otway Venice Preserv'd|page=4|passage=Home I would go, <br> But that my Dores are hatefull to my eyes. <br> Fill'd and damm'd up with gaping Creditors, <br> Watchfull as Fowlers when their Game will '''ſpring'''; {{...}}}}
## To [[grow]], to sprout.
##* {{RQ:KJV|Job|38|25–27|passage=Who hath [[divided|diuided]] a water-course for the [[overflowing|ouerflowing]] of waters? or a way for the lightning of thunder,<br>To cause it to [[rain]]e on the earth, where no man is: on the [[wilderness]]e wherein there is no man?<br>To [[satisfy|satisfie]] the desolate and waste ground, and to cause the bud of the tender [[herb]]e to '''spring''' forth.}}
##* {{RQ:Shelley Queen Mab|page=61|passage=Commerce! beneath whose poison-breathing shade <br> No solitary virtue dares to '''spring''', {{...}} }}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|author=w:Dale Carnegie|title=w:How to Win Friends and Influence People|page=42
|passage=[[:w:Sigmund Freud|Dr. Sigmund Freud]]... says that everything you and I do '''springs''' from two motives: the sex urge and the desire to be great.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1974|author=w:James Albert Michener|title=Centennial|page=338
|passage=There was moisture in the ground, and from it '''sprang''' a million flowers, gold and blue and brown and red.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=N. Roberts|title=Morrigann's Cross|section=vi
|passage=Foxglove '''sprang''' tall and purple among the trees.}}
### {{lb|en|UK dialectal}} To mature.
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[arise]], to [[come]] into [[existence]].
##: {{syn|en|arise|form|take shape}}
## {{lb|en|sometimes figurative}} To [[enliven]].
##: {{ux|en|He hit the gas and the car [[spring to life|'''sprang''' to life]].}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|usually with cardinal adverbs}} To [[move]] with [[great]] [[speed]] and [[energy]].
##: {{syn|en|bound|jump|leap}}
##: {{ux|en|Deer '''spring''' with their hind legs, using their front hooves to steady themselves.}}
##* {{c.|1250}}, ''Life of St Margaret'', [http://www.lel.ed.ac.uk/ihd/laeme2/tagged_data/tr323bt.html Trin. Col. MS B.14.39 (323), f. 22v:]
##*: ...into [[hell]]e '''spring'''...
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1474|translator=William Caxton|title=Game and Playe of the Chesse|section=iii, vii, 141
|passage=[[the|Ye]] [[king|kynge]]... '''sprange''' out of his [[chair|chare]] and [[received|resseyuyd]] them worshipfully.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1722|author=w:Ambrose Philips|title=The Briton
|passage=...the Mountain Stag, that '''springs'''<br>From Height to Height, and bounds along the Plains,<br>Nor has a Master to restrain his Course...}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1827|author=w:Clement Clarke Moore|title={{a|en|A Visit from St. Nicholas}}
|passage=...out on the lawn there arose such a clatter,<br>I '''sprang''' from my bed to see what was the matter.}}
##* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=1|passage=However, with the dainty volume my quondam friend '''sprang''' into fame. At the same time he cast off the chrysalis of a commonplace existence.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1912|author=w:Edgar Rice Burroughs| title=w:Tarzan of the Apes| chapter=5| passage=Thus she advanced; her belly low, almost touching the surface of the ground&mdash;a great cat preparing to '''spring''' upon its prey.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 11 2011|journal=The Atlantic
|passage=Reporters '''sprang''' to the conclusion that the speech would make detailed new commitments...}}
## {{lb|en|usually with from}} To [[be born]], [[descend]], or [[originate]] from
##: {{ux|en|He '''sprang''' from peasant stock.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=George McCandless|title=The ABCs of RBCs|publisher=Harvard University Press|page=7
|passage=From this basis, a first-order difference equation for the evolution of capital per worker is found, and the time path of the economy '''springs''' from this equation.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[rise]] in [[social]] [[position]] or [[military]] [[rank]], to be [[promoted]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[cause]] to spring (all senses).
## {{lb|en|of mechanisms}} To [[cause]] to [[work]] or [[open]] by [[sudden]] [[application]] of [[pressure]].
##: {{ux|en|He '''sprang''' the trap.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1625|author=Samuel Purchas|title=Purchas His Pilgrimes|section=Vol. II, x, ix
|passage=They '''sprung''' another Mine... wherein was placed about [[sixty|sixtie]] Barrels of Powder.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1747|title=The London Magazine, Or, Gentleman's Monthly Intelligencer
|passage=On the 23d, the Besiegers '''sprung''' a Mine under the Salient Angle, upon the Right of the Haif Moon, which had the desired Success, the Enemy's Gallery on that Side, and the Mason-Work of the Counterscarp, being thereby demolished.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To leap over.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1876|author=Matilda Leathes|title=Our village worthies; or, Stories of village life|page=112
|passage=I '''sprang''' the fence, and was soon in the village street.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|of horses}} To [[breed]] with, to [[impregnate]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1585|translator=Thomas Washington|author=Nicolas De Nicolay|title=The Navigations, Peregrinations, and Voyages, Made into Turkie...|section=Bk. IV, p. 154
|passage=...[they] sought the fairest stoned horses to '''spring''' their mares...}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[wet]], to [[moisten]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|now usually with "apart" or "open"}} To [[burst]] into [[piece]]s, to [[explode]], to [[shatter]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1698|author=François Froger|title=A Relation of a Voyage Made... on the Coasts of Africa|page=30
|passage=On the 22nd the mines '''sprang''', and took very good effect.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|military}} To [[go off]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=April 21 2012|journal=Sydney Morning Herald|page=5
|passage=The whole [[contraption]] appears liable to '''spring''' apart at any moment.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|nautical|usually perfective}} To [[crack]].
#* '''1582''' August 2, Richard Madox, diary:
#*: The ''Edward'' '''sprang''' [[her|hir]] [[foremast]].
# To [[come upon]] and [[flush out]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1921|title=Field and Stream|page=832
|text=For generations of men the springer spaniel has been looked upon as the dog for '''springing''' pheasants in covert and finding and retrieving dead birds or winged runners when ordered to do so. The properly broken dog will not chase, but drop to wing and shot.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1940|author=Allen A. Day|title=Dachsunds for Woodchucks|editor=Dwight Williams Huntington|work=The Game Breeder and Sportsman|page=94
|text={{...}} by the beginning of this century a still smaller breed, with a weight of 4 of 5 pounds and a chest measurement of around 12 inches, had come into being for '''springing''' rabbits. Such, then, is a rough, quick ancestral picture of our modern Dachshund, and{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2003-08-01|author=Dennis Walrod|title=Grouse Hunter's Guide: Solid Facts, Insights, and Observations on How to Hunt Ruffled Grouse|publisher=Stackpole Books|isbn=9780811743020
|text=I winter, ruffed grouse sometimes roost at night on the ground under the insulating snow. Even during the midday hours, I have often flushed grouse out from under the snow-bowed branches of "buck-brush," the type of environment where a hunter would more likely expect to '''spring''' a rabbit or two.}}
# {{lb|en|Australia|slang}} To [[catch]] in an [[illegal]] [[act]] or [[compromising]] [[position]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1980|author=John Hepworth; et al|title=Boozing Out in Melbourne Pubs...|page=42
|passage=He figured that nobody would ever '''spring''' him, but he figured wrong.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[begin]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|slang}} To [[put]] [[bad]] [[money]] into [[circulation]].
# To [[tell]], to [[share]].
#: {{ux|en|Sorry to '''spring''' it on you like this but I've been offered another job.}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |date=29 February 2012 |author=Aidan Foster-Carter |work=BBC News |url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-17213948 |title=North Korea: The Denuclearisation Dance Resumes |passage=[[North Korea]] loves to '''spring''' surprises. More unusual is for its US foe to play along.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260825102125/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-asia-17213948|archivedate=25 August 2026}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang|US}} To [[free]] from [[imprisonment]], [[especially]] by [[facilitating]] an [[illegal]] [[escape]].
#: {{syn|en|free|let out|release|spring loose|jailbreak}}
#: {{ux|en|His lieutenants hired a team of miners to help '''spring''' him.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=2007|passage=If I was in jail I know you'd '''spring''' me|author=w:Mike Batt|artist=w:Katie Melua|title=w:If You Were a Sailboat}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang|rare}} To be [[free]] of [[imprisonment]], [[especially]] by [[illegal]] [[escape]].
# To secure a person's release from [[custody]]; to free or [[bail out]] of jail.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1954|year_published=1957|author=w:Ian Fleming|title={{w|Live and Let Die (novel)|Live and Let Die}}|volume=|location=London|publisher=Pan Books|chapter=A Visiting-Card|page=22|passage=The Big Man, of course, had had an alibi as solid as Fort Knox. He had been held and questioned, but was quickly '''sprung''' by the best lawyer in Harlem.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|architecture|of arches}} To [[build]], to [[form]] the [[initial]] [[curve]] of.
#: {{ux|en|They '''sprung''' an arch over the lintel.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|architecture|of arches|with "from"}} To [[extend]], to [[curve]].
#: {{ux|en|The arches '''spring''' from the front posts.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|nautical}} To [[turn]] a [[vessel]] [[using]] a [[#Noun|spring]] [[attach]]ed to its [[anchor]] [[cable]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[pay]] or [[spend]] a [[certain]] [[sum]], to [[yield]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1957|author=w:Pelham Grenville Wodehouse|title=Over Seventy|page=137
|passage=He wouldn't '''spring''' a nickel for a bag of peanuts.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive|slang}} To [[raise]] an [[offered]] [[price]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|US|dialectal}} {{alt form|en|sprain}}.
# {{lb|en|transitive|US|dialectal}} {{alt form|en|strain}}.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[act]] as a [[#Noun|spring]]: to [[strongly]] [[rebound]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|rare}} To [[equip]] with [[#Noun|spring]]s, [[especially]] {{lb|en|of|_|vehicles}} to [[equip]] with a [[suspension]].
# {{lb|en|figurative|rare|obsolete}} to [[inspire]], to [[motivate]].
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[deform]] [[owing]] to [[excessive]] [[pressure]], to [[become]] [[warped]]; to [[intentionally]] [[deform]] [[in order to]] [[position]] and [[then]] [[straighten]] [[in place]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1873|month=July|journal=Routledge's Young Gentleman's Magazine|page=503
|passage=Don't drive it in too hard, as it will ‘'''spring'''’ the plane-iron, and make it concave.}}
#: {{ux|en|A piece of timber sometimes '''springs''' in seasoning.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''sprang''' in the slat.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|UK|dialectal|chiefly of cows}} To [[swell]] with [[milk]] or [[pregnancy]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1955|author=w:Patrick White|title=w:The Tree of Man|url=https://archive.org/details/treeofmannovel00whit/page/228/mode/1up?q=springing|chapter=15|page=228|publisher=Viking|location=New York
|passage=“Gee, Dad, Nancy’s '''springing''' all right,” Ray said and paused in spontaneous pleasure.<br>Stan Parker came, and together they looked at their swelling heifer.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of rattles|archaic}} To [[sound]], to [[play]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1850|author=Samuel Prout Newcombe|title=Pleasant pages|page=197|passage=I do not know how John and his mistress would have settled the fate of the thief, but just at this moment a policeman entered — for the cook had '''sprung''' the rattle, and had been screaming "Murder" and "Thieves."}}
# {{lb|en|of animals}} To [[find]] or [[get]] [[enough]] [[food]] during [[springtime]].

=====Usage notes=====
* The past-tense forms {{m|en|sprang}} and {{m|en|sprung}} are both well attested historically. In modern usage, {{m|en||sprang}} is comparatively formal (and more often considered correct), {{m|en||sprung}} comparatively informal. The past participle, however, is overwhelmingly {{m|en||sprung}}; {{m|en||sprang|}} as a past participle is attested, but is no longer in standard use.

