===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|spryng|t=a [[wellspring]], [[tide]], [[branch]], [[sunrise]], [[kind]] of [[dance]] or [[blow]], [[ulcer]], [[snare]], [[flock]]}}; partly from {{inh|en|ang|spring|t=[[wellspring]], [[ulcer]]}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*spring}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*springaz|t=a wellspring, fount}}; and partly from {{inh|en|ang|spryng||a [[jump]]}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*sprungi}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sprungiz|t=a jump}}. Further senses derived from the verb and from clippings of {{m|en|day-spring}}, {{m|en|springtime}}, {{m|en|spring tide}}, etc. Its sense as the season, first attested in a work predating 1325, gradually replaced Middle English  {{m|enm|lenten}}, {{m|enm|lente}}, from Old English {{m|ang|lencten||spring, [[Lent]]}} as that word became more specifically liturgical. Compare {{m|en|fall}}.

====Noun====
{{wikidata lexeme|L3334}}
[[File:Frühlingsallee Tulpenblüte 2010 (1).jpg|thumb|'''Spring''' (season) in Germany]]
[[Image:Coil_spring.JPG|thumb|right|A coil '''spring''' (mechanical device)]]
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} An [[act]] of [[springing]]: a [[leap]], a [[jump]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1700|author=w:John Dryden|url=https://www.poetrynook.com/poem/cock-and-fox-1|title=The Cock and the Fox
|passage=The [[prisoner|pris'ner]] with a '''spring''' from prison broke;<br>Then [[stretched|stretch'd]] his [[feathered|feather'd]] fans with all his might,<br>And to the [[neighboring|neighb'ring]] maple [[winged|wing'd]] his flight.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q1312}}{{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} The [[season]] of the [[year]] in [[temperate]] [[region]]s in which temperatures and daylight hours rise, and [[plant]]s [[#Verb|spring]] from the [[ground]] and into [[bloom]] and [[dormant]] [[animal]]s [[spring to life]].
#: {{syn|en|springtime}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|summer|[[autumn]] or [[fall]]|winter}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Spring''' is the time of the year most species reproduce.}}
#: {{ux|en|You can visit me in the '''spring''', when the weather is bearable.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXXVIII|59|No joy the blowing season gives,<br>⁠The herald melodies of '''spring''',<br>⁠But in the songs I love to sing<br>A doubtful gleam of solace lives.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title={{w|The Love Cats (song)|The Lovecats}}|artist=w:The Cure|year=1983|author={{w|Robert Smith (musician)|Robert Smith}}|passage=Into the sea, you and me / All these years and no one heard / I'll show you in '''spring''', it's a treacherous thing}}
#* {{quote-journal |en |year=2012 |month=March-April |author=Anna Lena Phillips |title=Sneaky Silk Moths |volume=100 |issue=2 |page=172 |magazine=w:American Scientist |url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/sneaky-silk-moths |passage=Last '''spring''', the periodical cicadas emerged across eastern North America. Their vast numbers and short above-ground life spans inspired awe and irritation in humans—and made for good meals for birds and small mammals. |accessdate=24 May 2013 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130219220644/https://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/sneaky-silk-moths |archivedate=19 February 2013 }}
## {{lb|en|astronomy}} The [[period]] from the [[moment]] of [[vernal equinox]] ([[around]] [[March]] 21 in the [[Northern Hemisphere]]) to the [[moment]] of the [[summer solstice]] ([[around]] [[June]] 21); [[:w:Spring (season)|the equivalent periods]] [[reckon]]ed in [[other]] [[culture]]s and [[calendar]]s.
##: {{ux|en|[[Chinese New Year]] always occurs in January or February but is called the "[[Spring Festival|'''Spring''' Festival]]" throughout [[East Asia]] because it is reckoned as the beginning of their '''spring'''.}}
## {{lb|en|meteorology}} The [[three]] [[month]]s of [[March]], [[April]], and [[May]] in the [[Northern Hemisphere]] and [[September]], [[October]], and [[November]] in the [[Southern Hemisphere]].
##: {{ux|en|I spent my '''spring''' holidays in Morocco.}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''spring''' issue will be out next week.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|figurative}} The [[time]] of [[something]]'s [[growth]]; the [[early]] [[stage]]s of [[some]] [[process]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|1 Samuel|9|26|passage=...and it came to [[pass]]e about the '''spring''' of the day, that Samuel called Saul to the top of the house...}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|1|3|passage=O how this '''spring''' of love resembleth<br>The uncertain glory of an April day.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|politics}} A [[period]] of [[political]] [[liberalization]] and [[democratization]].
##: {{ux|en|[['''Arab''' Spring]]}}
##: {{ux|en|{{w|'''Prague''' Spring}}}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:National Geographic|year=1989|month=May|volume=175|number=5|title=The Baltic: Arena of Power|author=Priit J. Vesilind|page=627
|text=Solidarity grew from the Lenin Shipyard here and threatened a Polish '''spring''' before martial law was imposed in 1981.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|fashion}} Someone with ivory or peach skin tone and eyes and hair that are not extremely dark, seen as best suited to certain colors of clothing.
# {{lb|en|countable}} [[something|Something]] which [[#Verb|spring]]s, springs forth, [[springs up]], or [[springs back]], ''[[particularly]]''
## {{senseid|en|Q124714}} {{lb|en|geology}} A [[spray]] or [[body]] of [[water]] [[#Verb|springing]] from the [[ground]].
##: {{syn|en|fount|source}}
##: {{ux|en|This beer was brewed with pure [[spring water|'''spring''' water]].}}
## {{lb|en|oceanography|obsolete}} The [[rising]] of the [[sea]] at [[high tide]].
## {{lb|en|oceanography}} {{ellipsis of|en|spring tide}}, the [[especially]] [[high]] [[tide]] [[shortly]] after [[full moon|full]] and [[new moon]]s.
##: {{ant|en|neap tide}}
## {{senseid|en|Q102836}} An [[elastic#Adjective|elastic]] mechanical part or device in any shape (e.g., [[flat#Adjective|flat]], [[curved]], [[coiled]]), made of [[flexible]]  [[material]] (usually [[spring steel]]) that [[exert]]s [[force]] and [[attempt]]s to [[spring back]] when [[bent]], [[compress]]ed, or [[stretch]]ed.
##: {{ux|en|We jumped so hard the bed '''springs''' broke.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[line]] from a [[vessel]]'s [[end]] or [[side]] to its [[anchor]] [[cable]] [[use]]d to [[diminish]] or [[control]] its [[movement]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1836|author=Frederick Marryat|title=Mr. Midshipman Easy|volume=III|page=72
|passage=He had warped round with the '''springs''' on his cable, and had recommenced his fire upon the ''Aurora''.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[line]] [[laid out]] from a [[vessel]]'s [[end]] to the [[opposite]] [[end]] of an [[adjacent]] [[vessel]] or [[mooring]] to [[diminish]] or [[control]] its [[movement]].
##: {{ux|en|You should put a couple of '''springs''' onto the jetty to stop the boat moving so much.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1769|author=William Falconer|title=An Universal Dictionary of the Marine|section=s.v
|passage='''Spring''' is likewise a rope reaching diagonally from the stern of a ship to the head of another which lies along-side or a-breast of her.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 26 2007|journal=Business Times:
|passage=‘''Springs''’ are the ropes used on a ship that is alongside a berth to prevent fore and aft movements.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[race]], a [[lineage]].
## {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[youth]].
## A [[shoot]], a [[young]] [[tree]].
## A [[grove]] of [[tree]]s; a [[forest]].
## {{lb|en|collective}} A group of [[teal]].
