===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=normal|:inh|enm:standard}}

From {{inh|en|enm|standard}}, from {{der|en|fro|estandart|t=gathering place, battle flag}}, from {{der|en|frk|*standahard|lit=stand firm, stand hard}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|stand|ard|nocat=1}}. An alternative etymology derives the second element from {{der|en|frk|*oʀd|t=point, spot, place}} (compare {{cog|fro|ordé|t=pointed}}, {{cog|ang|ord|t=point, source, vanguard}}, {{cog|de|Standort|t=location, place, site, position, base|lit=standing-point}}). Merged with {{inh|en|enm|standar}}, {{m|enm|stander}}, {{m|enm|standere|t=flag, banner|lit=stander}}, equivalent to {{af|en|stand|-er|id2=agent noun}}. More at {{l|en|stand}}, {{l|en|hard}}, {{l|en|ord}}.

As a hill-naming term possibly a {{cal|en|xcb|-|nocap=1|nocat=1}}; equivalent to {{cog|cy|lluman||standard}}, arising with confusion with the hill-naming element {{m|cy|llumon||chimney}}.<ref>{{cite-book|en|author=William John Watson|title=Celtic Place-Names of Scotland|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Birlinn|year=1926|year2=2004|isbn=9781841583235|page2=212}}</ref>

