===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|en|de|Statistik}}, from {{der|en|la-new|statisticum|t=of the state}} and {{der|en|it|statista|t=statesman, politician}}, compare {{cog|en|statist}}. ''Statistik'' introduced by {{w|Gottfried Achenwall}} (1749), originally designated the analysis of data about the state.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{senseid|en|Q12483}}A [[discipline]], principally within [[applied mathematics]], concerned with the systematic study of the collection, presentation, analysis, and interpretation of [[data]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Statistics''' is the only mathematical field required for many social sciences.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1972|author={{w|Leonard Jimmie Savage|Leonard J. Savage}}|title=The Foundations of '''Statistics'''|publisher=Dover|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=zSv6dBWneMEC&pg=PA1&dq=%22statistics%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiCneWrpaztAhUBTKwKHar5B_cQ6AEwCHoECGAQAg#v=onepage&q=%22statistics%22&f=false|page=1
|passage=As for '''statistics''', the foundations include, on any interpretation of which I have ever heard, the foundations of probability, as controversial a subject as one could name. As in other sciences, controversies over the foundations of '''statistics''' reflect themselves to some extent in everyday practice, nut not nearly so catastrophically as one might imagine.{{...}}It is hard to judge, however, to what extent the relative calm of modern '''statistics''' is due to its domination by a vigorous school relatively well agreed within itself about the foundations.}}
#* '''2004''', David C. LeBlanc, '''''Statistics''': Concepts and Applications for Science'', {{w|Jones and Bartlett Publishers}}, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=gtawVU0oZFMC&pg=PA61&dq=%22statistics%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiCneWrpaztAhUBTKwKHar5B_cQ6AEwB3oECGEQAg#v=onepage&q=%22statistics%22&f=false page 61],
#*: The application of '''statistics''' in the process of science can be divided into three parts: (1) obtaining data (experiment and sampling design), (2) summarizing and describing data (exploratory data analysis, descriptive '''statistics'''), and (3) using data from samples and experiments to make estimates and test competing hypotheses about the universe (inferential '''statistics''').
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=2012-01
|author=Robert L. Dorit
|title=Rereading Darwin
|volume=100
|issue=1
|page=23
|magazine=w:American Scientist
|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/rereading-darwin
|passage=We live our lives in three dimensions for our threescore and ten allotted years. Yet every branch of contemporary science, from '''statistics''' to cosmology, alludes to processes that operate on scales outside of human experience: the millisecond and the nanometer, the eon and the light-year.
|accessdate=11 May 2013
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20121114141738/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/rereading-darwin
