===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=right side|:inh|enm:sterbord<ety:inh<ang:stēorbord>>|tree=1}}
The {{lg|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|sterbord}}, {{m|enm|stere-bourd}}, {{m|enm|stere-burd|t=starboard}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|subentry=[stẹ̄r] bord|entry=stẹ̄r(e|pos=''n.''(2)|noformat=1|id=MED42827}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|stēorbord|t=right side of the ship looking forward, starboard}},<ref>{{R:Anglo-Saxon Dictionary|entry=steór-bord|page=917|column=2}}</ref> from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*steurubord}}, from {{m|gmw-pro|*stiurijan|t=to direct, steer}} (from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stiurijaną|t=to direct, steer}}, possibly ultimately from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*(s)twerH-|t=to agitate, stir up; to propel; to urge on}}) + {{m|gmw-pro|*bord|t=board, plank}} (from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*burdą|t=board, plank}}; further etymology uncertain, possibly from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*bʰerH-|t=to pierce; to strike}} or {{m|ine-pro|*bʰers-|t=point; tip; top|pos=n}}), referring to early Germanic ships where the rudder or steering oar was set to the right side to accommodate right-handed crew.<ref name="OED">Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=n.'', ''adj.'', and ''adv|id=5792766547|date=June 2026|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref><ref>As such a ship had to dock on its left side because the steering oar on the right would get in the way, the left side became known as the [[port#Noun|port]] side: {{cite-web|title=Why Do Ships Use ‘Port’ and ‘Starboard’ instead of ‘Left’ and ‘Right?’|work={{w|National Ocean Service}}, {{w|National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718174931/https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/port-starboard.html|accessdate=18 July 2026}}</ref> {{surf|en|steer|pos1=v|board|pos2=n|t2=side (of a ship)}}.

The {{lg|adverb}}, {{lg|adjective}},<ref name="OED"/> and {{lg|verb}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=1115539476|date=June 2026|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> are derived from the noun.

