# {{lb|en|[[heading]]}} ''A pile.''
## A large [[pile]] of [[hay]], [[grain]], [[straw]], or the like, larger at the bottom than the top, sometimes covered with [[thatch]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1790|author=w:William Cowper|title=The Needless Alarm
|passage=But corn was housed, and beans were in the '''stack'''.}}
## A pile of similar objects, each directly on [[top]] of the last.
##: {{ux|en|Please bring me a chair from that '''stack''' in the corner.}}
## {{lb|en|UK}} A pile of [[pole]]s or [[wood]], indefinite in quantity.
##* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|edition=3rd|century=III|chapter=Experiments in Consort Touching the Reflexion, of Sounds|page=67|para=249|passage=There was againſt euery Pillar, a '''Stacke''' of Billets, aboue a Mans Height;}}
## A pile of wood containing 108 [[cubic foot|cubic feet]]. (~3 m&sup3;)
## An extensive collection
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1997|author=Guy Claxton|title=Hare brain, tortoise mind: why intelligence increases when you think less
|passage=She performed appallingly on standard neurological tests, which are, as Sacks perceptively notes, specifically designed to deconstruct the whole person into a '''stack''' of &#39;abilities&#39;.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Elizabeth McLeod|title=The Original Amos 'n' Andy: Freeman Gosden, Charles Correll and the 1928-1943 Radio Serial|publisher=McFarland|isbn=9780786445844|page=26
|passage=“We said, &#39;Maybe we could come up with a couple of characters doing jokes,&#39;” Correll recalled in 1972. “We had a whole '''stack''' of jokes we used to do in these home talent shows}}
##* '''2007''', Great Britain: Parliament: House of Commons: Education and Skills Committee, ''Post-16 skills: ninth report of session 2006-07, Vol. 2: Oral and written evidence'', The Stationery Office {{ISBN|9780215036032}}, page 42
##*: Going back to an earlier question, which I think is very important, this question of how you use skills. It is no good having a great '''stack''' of skills in a workplace if the employer does not utilise them properly
# A [[smokestack]].
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=A Lady in Company|text=With just the turn of a shoulder she indicated the water front, where, at the end of the dock on which they stood, lay the good ship, ''Mount Vernon'', river packet, the black smoke already pouring from her '''stacks'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1949|month=January and February|author=F. G. Roe|title=I Saw Three Englands–1|journal=Railway Magazine|page=12|text=Long before Shap platform showed up around a corner and the two arms on the gradient post drooped in both directions at once, ''Duchess of Buccleuch's'' amiable throbbing purr at the '''stack''' had become a fierce freight-engine bark, as she resolutely dragged at her enormous load.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=July|author=J. Geoffrey Todd|title=Impressions of railroading in the United States: Part Two|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=419|text=The leading engine was one of the Class Y6 [[2-8-8-2]] compound articulateds, [...] The '''stack''' noise of one of these great brutes slogging up a grade was quite unforgettable.}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''In computing.''
## {{senseid|en|Q177929}} {{lb|en|data structure}} A linear [[data structure]] in which [[item]]s inserted are removed in reverse order (the last item inserted is the first one to be removed).
##: {{hypo|en|history stack}}
## {{lb|en|data structure|often with "the"}} A stack [[data structure]] stored in [[main memory]] that is manipulated during [[machine language]] [[procedure call]] related instructions.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Michael A. Miller|title=The 68000 Microprocessor Family: Architecture, Programming, and Applications|page=47|passage=When the microprocessor decodes the JSR opcode, it stores the operand into the TEMP register and pushes the current contents of the PC ($00 0128) onto the '''stack'''.}}
## {{lb|en|networking}} An [[implementation]] of a protocol [[suite]] (set of protocols forming a layered architecture).
##: {{syn|en|protocol stack}}
##: {{ux|en|A TCP/IP '''stack''' is a library or set of libraries or of OS drivers that take care of networking.}}
## A combination of [[interdependent]], yet individually replaceable, [[software]] components or technologies used together on a system.
##: {{syn|en|technology stack}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=John Paul Mueller|title=AWS For Admins For Dummies|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|isbn=9781119312499|page=323|passage=A Linux, Apache, MySQL, and PHP (LAMP) '''stack''' is a configuration of four popular products for hosting websites.}}
