===Etymology 1===
{{wp|The Staple}}
{{ety|en|id=supply|:inh|enm:staple|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|staple}}, from {{der|en|xno|estaple}}, {{der|en|fro|estaple||market, (trading) post}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|stapula}}, from {{der|en|frk|*stapul}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gem-pro|*stapulaz||post}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*stebʰ-||post, stem}}. Compare {{m|en|staff}}. {{doublet|en|étape}} and [[#Etymology 2|etymology 2]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|now|_|historical}} A [[town]] containing merchants who have exclusive [[right]], under [[royal]] [[authority]], to purchase or [[produce]] [[certain]] [[goods]] for [[export]]; [[also]], the [[body]] of such merchants [[seen]] as a [[group]].
#* {{RQ:Arbuthnot Coins
|passage=The customs of Alexandria were very great, it having been the '''staple''' of the Indian trade.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Kenilworth|passage=For the increase of [[trade]] and the encouragement of the worthy burgesses of Woodstock, her majesty was minded to erect the town into a '''staple''' for wool.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Thomas Penn|title=Winter King|page=73|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012
|passage=Calais was one of the ‘principal treasures’ of the crown, of both strategic and economic importance. It was home to the '''staple''', the crown-controlled marketplace for England's lucrative textile trade, whose substantial customs and tax revenues flooded into Henry's coffers.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Place of supply; source.
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=3|passage=Whitehall naturally became the [[chief]] '''staple''' of [[news]]. Whenever there was a rumour that anything important had happened or was about to happen, people hastened thither to obtain intelligence from the fountain head.}}
# The principal commodity produced in a town or region.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=October|title=Editorial: The importance of the "Roadrailer"|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=577|text=The old '''staple''' of coal is a declining traffic; and what remains tends to be hauled a shorter distance, as new power stations are sited closer to coalfields.}}
# A [[basic]] or [[essential]] [[supply]].
#: {{ux|en|Rice is a '''staple''' in the diet of many cultures.}}
# A recurring topic, character, or item.
#: {{ux|en|Lurid stories are a '''staple''' of tabloid journalism.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2010|journal=The Economist|section=Jul-Aug 2010, p. 27
|passage=In [[most]] countries, rubbish makes headlines only [[when]] it is not collected, and stinking [[sacks]] [[lie]] [[heaped]] on the streets. In Britain bins are a front-page '''staple'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/mtv-shuts-down-final-music-only-channels-with-video-killed-the-radio-star-3920577|date=1-1-2026|work=NME|author=Max Pilley|title=MTV shuts down final music-only channels with ‘Video Killed The Radio Star’|passage=MTV quickly became a staple of pop culture after its launch, and was tied to countless historic moments in music, including the world premiere of Michael Jackson’s ‘Thriller’ video and the 16-hour broadcast of the Live Aid concerts in 1985.}}
# Short fiber, as of cotton, sheep’s [[wool]], or the [[like]], which can be [[spun]] into [[yarn]] or [[thread]].
#: {{ux|en|[[tow|Tow]] is [[flax]] with short '''staple'''.}}
# Unmanufactured material; [[raw]] material.

