===Etymology 2===
[[Image:Copper Staples No 50,60.JPG|thumb|A box of staples]]
[[File:Krampen.jpg|thumb|fence staples]]
{{ety|en|id=fastener|:inh|enm:stapel|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|stapel||staple, pillar, post}}, from {{inh|en|ang|stapol||post, pillar}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*stapul}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*stapulaz}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*stebʰ-||post, stem}}.

See also {{cog|ang|steppan||to step}} and {{cog|fro|estaple||post}}. Consider also {{m|en|stapes||stirrup}}, from {{der|en|la|-}}. {{doublet|en|étape}} and [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q377164}} Any of several types of fastener comprising a bent piece of wire.
## A [[wire]] [[fastener]], made of thin wire, used to [[secure]] [[stack]]s of [[paper]] by [[penetrate|penetrating]] all the sheets and [[curl]]ing around.
##: {{ux|en|They stapled the documents with an office stapler, putting a '''staple''' in the top left corner of each one.}}
## A wire fastener, in any of various sizes, used to secure something else by penetrating and curling.
##: {{ux|en|They stapled the housewrap with a staple gun firing large '''staples'''.}}
## A U-shaped wire fastener, made of thick wire, used to attach fence wire or other material to posts or structures.
##: {{ux|en|The rancher used '''staples''' to attach the barbed wire to the fence posts.}}
##* {{RQ:Douglass Bondage|chapter=3|passage=Esther's wrists were firmly tied, and the twisted rope was fastened to a strong '''staple''' in a heavy wooden joist above, near the fire-place. Here she stood, on a bench, her arms tightly drawn over her breast. Her back and shoulders were bare to the waist.}}
# One of a set of U-shaped metal [[rod]]s hammered into a structure, such as a [[piling]] or [[wharf]], which serve as a [[ladder]].
#: {{ux|en|Fortunately, there were '''staples''' in the quay wall, and she was able to climb out of the water.}}
# A metal loop used to fasten doors in conjunction with a [[hasp]] and a padlock.
# {{lb|en|mining}} A [[shaft]], smaller and shorter than the principal one, joining different [[level]]s.
# A small [[pit]].
# A [[district]] granted to an [[abbey]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1605|author=w:William Camden|title=Remaines Concerning Britain|chapter=Money|url=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/Remains_Concerning_Britain/56YbAQAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0|passage=[Henry II] also granted liberty of coyning to certain Cities and Abbies, allowing them one '''staple''' and two puncheons at a rate, with certain restrictions.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[post]]; [[prop]]; [[support]]

